=== Module 21 - Nullable & FIRST Sets (least fixed points) ===

NULLABLE: start empty; add A when  A -> epsilon  or  A -> X1..Xk with every Xi
          already nullable; repeat until a pass adds nothing.
FIRST   : start empty; fold FIRST(rhs) into FIRST(lhs) for every production;
          repeat to convergence. Circular by nature -> needs the iteration.

########## fixtures/indirect.grammar

  (0) X -> Y
  (1) Y -> Z
  (2) Z -> z
  (3) Z -> epsilon

NULLABLE iteration:
  round 0: {  }
  round 1: { Z }
  round 2: { Y Z }
  round 3: { X Y Z }
  round 4: { X Y Z }   (stable -- one more pass adds nothing)
  => nullable nonterminals: { X Y Z }

FIRST iteration:
  round 0:
      FIRST(X ) = {  }
      FIRST(Y ) = {  }
      FIRST(Z ) = {  }
  round 1:
      FIRST(X ) = { epsilon }
      FIRST(Y ) = { epsilon }
      FIRST(Z ) = { z epsilon }
  round 2:
      FIRST(X ) = { epsilon }
      FIRST(Y ) = { z epsilon }
      FIRST(Z ) = { z epsilon }
  round 3:
      FIRST(X ) = { z epsilon }
      FIRST(Y ) = { z epsilon }
      FIRST(Z ) = { z epsilon }
  round 4:
      FIRST(X ) = { z epsilon }
      FIRST(Y ) = { z epsilon }
      FIRST(Z ) = { z epsilon }
  one more pass changes nothing? true

cross-check vs Module 20 Predict.firstOf: MATCH

########## fixtures/cascade.grammar

  (0) S -> A B C d
  (1) A -> a
  (2) A -> epsilon
  (3) B -> b
  (4) B -> epsilon
  (5) C -> c
  (6) C -> epsilon

NULLABLE iteration:
  round 0: {  }
  round 1: { A B C }
  round 2: { A B C }   (stable -- one more pass adds nothing)
  => nullable nonterminals: { A B C }

FIRST iteration:
  round 0:
      FIRST(S ) = {  }
      FIRST(A ) = {  }
      FIRST(B ) = {  }
      FIRST(C ) = {  }
  round 1:
      FIRST(S ) = { d }
      FIRST(A ) = { a epsilon }
      FIRST(B ) = { b epsilon }
      FIRST(C ) = { c epsilon }
  round 2:
      FIRST(S ) = { a b c d }
      FIRST(A ) = { a epsilon }
      FIRST(B ) = { b epsilon }
      FIRST(C ) = { c epsilon }
  round 3:
      FIRST(S ) = { a b c d }
      FIRST(A ) = { a epsilon }
      FIRST(B ) = { b epsilon }
      FIRST(C ) = { c epsilon }
  one more pass changes nothing? true

cross-check vs Module 20 Predict.firstOf: MATCH

########## fixtures/expr.grammar

  (0) E -> T Ep
  (1) Ep -> + T Ep
  (2) Ep -> epsilon
  (3) T -> F Tp
  (4) Tp -> * F Tp
  (5) Tp -> epsilon
  (6) F -> ( E )
  (7) F -> num
  (8) F -> id

NULLABLE iteration:
  round 0: {  }
  round 1: { Ep Tp }
  round 2: { Ep Tp }   (stable -- one more pass adds nothing)
  => nullable nonterminals: { Ep Tp }

FIRST iteration:
  round 0:
      FIRST(E ) = {  }
      FIRST(Ep) = {  }
      FIRST(T ) = {  }
      FIRST(Tp) = {  }
      FIRST(F ) = {  }
  round 1:
      FIRST(E ) = {  }
      FIRST(Ep) = { + epsilon }
      FIRST(T ) = {  }
      FIRST(Tp) = { * epsilon }
      FIRST(F ) = { ( id num }
  round 2:
      FIRST(E ) = {  }
      FIRST(Ep) = { + epsilon }
      FIRST(T ) = { ( id num }
      FIRST(Tp) = { * epsilon }
      FIRST(F ) = { ( id num }
  round 3:
      FIRST(E ) = { ( id num }
      FIRST(Ep) = { + epsilon }
      FIRST(T ) = { ( id num }
      FIRST(Tp) = { * epsilon }
      FIRST(F ) = { ( id num }
  round 4:
      FIRST(E ) = { ( id num }
      FIRST(Ep) = { + epsilon }
      FIRST(T ) = { ( id num }
      FIRST(Tp) = { * epsilon }
      FIRST(F ) = { ( id num }
  one more pass changes nothing? true

cross-check vs Module 20 Predict.firstOf: MATCH

why the LEAST fixed point?
  - the empty assignment is NOT a fixed point: the rules force elements in.
  - our result IS a fixed point: one more pass adds nothing (checked above).
  - we only ever ADD, starting from empty, so every element we put in was
    forced by a rule. Any other fixed point must also contain those elements.
    Therefore ours is contained in every fixed point = the least one.
  - the LEAST one is the right answer: a bigger set would claim a token can
    start a nonterminal when no derivation actually produces it there.
